AUDIO: 'You\'ve probably already heard about agent loops since everyone is hyping them up a lot right now. And you might think they\'re just a way for these AI companies to get you spending more on their products since these loops chew through a lot of tokens. But that only happens when you\'re using the wrong type'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of loop for the job. As you already know, we\'re a software company and we\'ve been experimenting with these loops in our AI coding tasks. Along the way, we\'ve mapped out the different types of loops and which use cases each one is'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually suited for. So, out of all the loops we\'ve set up, we\'re going to share the ones we found genuinely useful. We\'ll also show you how to set up each one and how each loop is going to impact your workflow. Before we get into the different types of loops, let\'s quickly'

[00:38] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A terminal window showing code specifications for a landing page, including design tokens and technical requirements.
AUDIO: 'recap what loop engineering actually is for those of you who are new here. We won\'t go deep here, but if you want the full breakdown, we covered it in a previous video on the channel. The core idea of loop engineering is that you stop being the person writing the'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompts that drive the agent and you turn it into a system that writes the loop. itself. Instead of spending your time setting things up and writing long, carefully structured prompts just to get it to build, you let the agent handle everything on its own. It learns as it'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'goes, grows from the problems it hits along the way, and figures out what it needs to do next. That\'s what an agent loop really is. In that previous video, we split loops into two types based on the outcome you get, the deterministic loop and the non-deterministic loop. A'

[01:17] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Animated icons representing different aspects of AI loops (time, lightning, shield).
AUDIO: 'deterministic loop is the kind where you already know the outcome. So the agent has a solid way to check its own work against it and it keeps working until it gets there. A non-deterministic loop is the kind where you don\'t. So there\'s no'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solid way for the agent to check its work which means you need other ways to handle it. But that was a broad split and we showed just one way to set each of them up. They can be built in a lot of different ways and each setup changes what you can do. The first type is one'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'most of you have probably already used. It\'s basically the building block of every other loop and the clearest example is the goal command. We call it the stateless loop and basically it means the loop doesn\'t hold on to anything or improve itself as it works.'

[01:55] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Claude Code terminal interface displaying a welcome message, version (v2.1.150), and the `/goal` command prompt.
AUDIO: 'There\'s no part where it learns from what happened and gets better. That\'s exactly what makes these the simplest loops there are. The one you\'ve probably already seen of this type is the Ralph loop and it\'s stateless because it never kept any memory. It just ran the same'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'task again and again and the moment it saw the task was finished, it stopped. As you might already know about the goal command, it is the best example of the stateless loop. To use it, you tell it what you want to build right after the'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'goal command. From there, Claude sets that as the goal and starts working on it. Then every time the main agent decides a task is done, it uses a smaller model to doublech checkck the work. In Claude code, that smaller model is haiku. It checks everything the agent'

[02:33] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A file explorer in a code editor showing a project structure with `actions.test.ts` files highlighted.
AUDIO: 'did against the requirements you gave in the prompt. And if the task isn\'t fully done, it reprompts the agent to finish what it missed. But there\'s a problem with this loop. It leans entirely on the model to decide whether a task\'s done with no standard to measure the work'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'against. That\'s why it works best on features whose requirements you can check in some hard concrete way. One way to do that is with tests. Like we talked about in our previous video, we write the tests before asking Claude to build'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any feature. That way, if Claude changes that feature in a way it shouldn\'t, the tests throw an error and tell Claude its implementation is off. And once we\'ve got tests for every feature, we can hand the agent real autonomy and let it work without worrying it\'ll break the other'

[03:12] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: An animation showing two commits with checkmarks, illustrating a version control process.
AUDIO: 'features or build the one we want the wrong way. Once you\'ve written the tests, you can ask Claude code to set the goal as getting that feature to pass all the tests and it keeps writing code. Running the tests to check itself and keeps going until every test passes.'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Once they all pass, that means the features built correctly and Claude will mark the goal as complete. Since the agent\'s working on its own, you\'ll want to add one line to your Claude. MD file. That line tells the agent to save every'

[03:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working version of the app. That way, if it breaks the app somewhere down the line, it can just roll back to the last version that worked and carry on from there instead of trying to undo changes from memory. But before we move on to more types, let\'s have a word by our'

